Patch 49. After patch 48.

The command processor's pending_fns_ was a std::queue without a lock. CallInThread pushes to it
from other threads (clearing caches, invalidating GPU memory, shader storage, pause, the swap's
post effect), while the command processor's thread checks it, takes calls from it and runs
them: a data race on the queue.

The queue is now rex::graphics::PendingCalls (include/rex/graphics/pending_calls.h): Push and
Empty take a mutex, and RunAll takes each call out under the mutex and runs it outside it, so a
call can queue another one and no lock is held while a call runs. CallInThread still runs a call
at once when it is on the command processor's thread and nothing is queued. It checks the thread
first: only that thread runs queued calls, so there an empty queue cannot fill with an earlier
call before this one runs. Calls run in the order they were queued, as before.

Test: tests/unit/graphics/pending_calls_test.cpp. Four threads each push 20,000 calls while
another thread drains the queue, as the command processor's loop does. Every call runs, and each
pushing thread's calls run in its order. A second test checks that a call queued by a running
call runs as well. Built alone with ThreadSanitizer (clang 21, Linux): three runs, no report.
The same test against a copy of the class with its locks removed: 61 data race reports, and the
run hangs on the corrupted queue. The SDK's tests pass with it (Linux: 1775).

